## Context
`scripts/seat_clock.py` `identities()` decides from the plan's free-text reason
column whether a `door=headless` worker header is the launcher's or a subagent's
forgery (Blind-Spot 1, adf4b6ea). PR 2094 (1388) let the header win when the
reason names no door, which reopened the forgery for reason `subagent`; the
follow-up (ticket 1396) untrusts the header when the reason says `subagent`.
A subagent seat with any other free-text reason can still forge a headless
header, and a forged identity corrupts the attribution records the route grid
reads.

Since 1384, `seat_clock.py run` appends one launch line per headless seat
(`launches.jsonl`): an observed fact, written by the launcher, not the model.

## Scope
- `identities()` trusts a `door=headless` header only when a launch line records
  that seat for this review (seat name and unit or panel); otherwise the seat
  takes the subagent identity. The plan's reason text stops deciding.
- An unreadable launch archive falls back to untrusted, with one warning.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] Test: a forged headless header with a free-text reason and no launch line is recorded `route=subagent`
- [ ] Test: a launcher-stamped seat with a launch line keeps its header identity whatever the reason says (1388 case)
- [ ] The reason-text checks of 1388 and 1396 are removed
